def shell(title,body,script):return f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#f9efeb"><title>{title} | Y9 Science | Learning Lab</title><meta name="description" content="Explore blood-vessel structure, blood components, oxygen and dissolved transport, defence and clotting through interactive models and a delivery game."><link rel="icon" href="{P}assets/brand/learning-lab-logo.png"><link rel="stylesheet" href="{P}assets/blood-vessels/blood.css?v=1"><script type="module" src="{P}assets/blood-vessels/{script}?v=game-records-1"></script></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to lesson</a><header class="blood-header"><a class="blood-brand" href="{P}index.html"><img src="{P}assets/brand/learning-lab-logo.png" width="43" height="43" alt="">learning<span>lab</span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="{P}index.html#/years/y9/science">Y9 Science</a><a href="./">Blood vessels lab</a><a href="revision.html">Revision simulator</a></nav></header><main id="main"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="{P}index.html">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="{P}index.html#/years/y9/science">Y9 Science</a><span>›</span><a href="./">Inside your blood vessels</a></nav>{body}</main><footer class="blood-footer"><span>Learning Lab · Y9 Science · Human biology</span><a href="../circulatory-system/">Explore the heart →</a><a href="../breathing-system/">Explore breathing →</a></footer></body></html>'''
def head(n,tag,title,desc=''):return f'<div class="section-head"><span class="section-number">{n:02}</span><div><span class="kicker">{tag}</span><h2>{title}</h2>{"<p>"+desc+"</p>" if desc else ""}</div></div>'
sources='''<details class="sources"><summary>Biology sources, game inspiration and model limits</summary><p>Original Learning Lab explanations, illustrations and game. Biology checked against OpenStax <cite>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</cite>, chapters 18 and 20, using its public textbook source. The further-reading links are optional; every instruction needed to learn and play is on this platform.</p><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/openstax/osbooks-anatomy-physiology/tree/main/modules/m46710" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenStax: an overview of blood</a> — composition, transport, regulation and protection.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/openstax/osbooks-anatomy-physiology/tree/main/modules/m46707" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenStax: erythrocytes</a> — haemoglobin and oxygen carriage.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/openstax/osbooks-anatomy-physiology/tree/main/modules/m46701" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenStax: leukocytes and platelets</a> and <a href="https://github.com/openstax/osbooks-anatomy-physiology/tree/main/modules/m46705" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">haemostasis</a> — immune functions, plug and fibrin.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/openstax/osbooks-anatomy-physiology/tree/main/modules/m46597" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenStax: vessel structure and function</a> and <a href="https://github.com/openstax/osbooks-anatomy-physiology/tree/main/modules/m46586" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">capillary exchange</a>.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/phetsims/membrane-channels/blob/main/doc/model.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PhET public membrane-transport model description</a> — inspiration for visible particles and qualitative gradient investigation.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/gunjanjaswal/BloodTypingGame#readme" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Blood Typing Game: public educational-game description</a> — inspiration for choosing a blood-related action and learning from its consequence. Our game teaches component transport, rather than transfusion compatibility.</li></ul><p>Cells and molecules are enlarged, cell counts are not proportional, and motion is slowed for visibility. Wall thickness, vessel size and capillary speed are qualitative comparisons. The transport map shows route order rather than anatomy; it does not predict clinical delivery times. The centrifuge is a pre-balanced, closed teaching model, not a blood-handling procedure.</p></details>'''
reading='''<div class="learn-grid"><article class="study-card"><h3>Blood is a living transport tissue.</h3><p>Blood flows through vessels, the tubes that connect the heart to body tissues. A tissue is a group of cells working together. Blood is unusual because its cells and fragments are suspended in a liquid called <strong>plasma</strong>. Its jobs include transport, defence, repair and regulation of the internal environment.</p><p>Cells need oxygen and nutrients such as glucose for aerobic respiration, a chemical process that transfers energy from food using oxygen. Blood also removes carbon dioxide and carries other waste towards organs that can excrete it. Circulation means the bulk movement of blood; exchange means substances passing between blood and surrounding tissue.</p><p class="word-key"><strong>Unpack the words:</strong> A nutrient is a substance needed for growth, repair or energy transfer. A waste is a product that needs removal or control. “Dissolved” means particles are distributed through a liquid at molecular or ionic scale, rather than visible grains or bubbles.</p><p class="memory"><strong>Remember: deliver, defend, seal, balance.</strong> Blood supplies materials, supports immune defence, seals damaged vessels and redistributes heat and water. Its components share these jobs.</p></article><article class="study-card"><h3>Four components, different jobs.</h3><p><strong>Red cells</strong> are flexible discs with a dip on each face. Haemoglobin inside them binds most transported oxygen. Mature human red cells lack a nucleus, leaving space for haemoglobin. The nucleus is the compartment containing most of a cell’s genetic material.</p><p><strong>Plasma</strong> is mostly water but also carries dissolved nutrients, ions, urea, hormones and proteins. An ion is an electrically charged particle. Urea is a nitrogen-containing waste made in the liver and excreted by the kidneys. A hormone is a chemical signal released into blood by an endocrine gland, an organ that releases these messages; only suitable target cells respond. Most carbon dioxide travels as bicarbonate ions in plasma, formed largely through reactions in red cells.</p><p><strong>White cells</strong> have nuclei and perform different immune roles. Some engulf (surround and take in) microbes such as bacteria; some lymphocytes develop into antibody-producing cells. Antibodies are proteins that recognise particular molecular targets. <strong>Platelets</strong> are small cell fragments that form a plug at damage; plasma clotting proteins help stabilise it with fibrin, a strand-forming protein.</p><p class="word-key"><strong>Size key:</strong> μm means micrometre, one millionth of a metre. A red cell is about 7–8 μm across. A biconcave shape has two inward dips; it increases surface area relative to volume and shortens diffusion distance. The pictures enlarge rare components so you can inspect them.</p></article><article class="study-card"><h3>A vessel’s structure suits its job.</h3><p>An <strong>artery</strong> carries blood away from the heart. Its muscular, elastic wall withstands and responds to higher pulsatile pressure: pressure rises and falls with pumping. Elastic means able to stretch and recoil. Smaller arteries, called arterioles, branch into capillary networks.</p><p>A <strong>capillary</strong> is a microscopic exchange vessel. Its lining, the <strong>endothelium</strong>, is one cell thick with a thin supporting layer. Red cells often squeeze through in single file. Capillaries join into venules and then <strong>veins</strong>, which return blood towards the heart at lower pressure. Many limb veins have valves; contracting nearby muscles can assist forward flow.</p><p class="word-key"><strong>Lumen</strong> means the space inside a vessel. A <strong>cross-section</strong> is a view cut across the tube; a longitudinal view looks along its length. <strong>Pressure</strong> is force per area. P = F/A: P is pressure in pascals (Pa), F perpendicular force in newtons (N), A area in square metres (m²), and 1 Pa = 1 N/m². This lab compares pressure patterns without pretending to measure a particular patient.</p><p class="memory"><strong>A = Away.</strong> Direction defines an artery. The pulmonary artery is oxygen-poor; the pulmonary vein is oxygen-rich. Pulmonary means relating to the lungs. All normal blood is red: oxygen-rich blood is brighter, oxygen-poor blood darker.</p></article><article class="study-card"><h3>Flow brings substances; exchange transfers them.</h3><p>In body tissues, cells use oxygen, helping maintain a lower oxygen partial pressure than in arriving blood. Oxygen leaves haemoglobin and diffuses towards the tissue. Carbon dioxide made in cells moves the other way. At the lungs these gas directions reverse as fresh air and flowing blood maintain the differences.</p><p><strong>Diffusion</strong> is net movement down a gradient caused by random particle motion. A gradient is a difference across a distance. For gases, partial pressure is the contribution of that gas to the mixture’s pressure. O₂ means an oxygen molecule with two oxygen atoms; CO₂ means carbon dioxide, with one carbon and two oxygen atoms. The arrows show net gas transfer, not whole red cells passing through the wall.</p><p>Small substances cross suitable exchange barriers by appropriate routes. Glucose needs transport proteins to enter many body cells. Most large plasma proteins normally remain in the blood. Some fluid leaves capillaries and returns through the lymphatic system, a drainage network that reconnects with blood; a capillary is not an open pipe leaking every component.</p><p class="word-key"><strong>Think about a journey:</strong> urea: liver → plasma → kidneys → urine. Oxygen: lungs → haemoglobin in red cells → body tissue. An arrow means “next stage”, not that these organs connect by a single direct vessel. Between body organs, blood normally returns through the right heart, lungs and left heart.</p></article></div>'''
body=f'''<section class="hero"><div><span class="kicker">YEAR 9 SCIENCE · STRUCTURE, TRANSPORT AND PROTECTION</span><h1>Inside your<br>blood vessels.</h1><p>A moving tissue. A network of living tubes. Discover how the shape of a red cell, the thickness of a vessel wall and the contents of plasma help keep your body supplied.</p><div class="hero-links"><a class="button primary" href="#vessel-lab">Enter the vessel lab ↓</a><a class="button" href="transport-game.html">Play Transport Dispatch →</a></div></div><img src="cover.svg" width="800" height="460" alt="Red blood cells with pale central dips, a nucleated white cell and a small platelet suspended in a cutaway vessel"></section><section id="learn-first">{head(1,'READ · PICTURE · EXPLAIN','Understand the system before you investigate.','Start with a familiar journey: delivering a useful substance and taking a waste away.')}{reading}</section><section id="vessel-lab">{head(2,'PREDICT · OBSERVE · COMPARE','Step inside a living vessel.','Choose a vessel. Click a moving cell or the plasma, then connect its structure with its function.')}<div class="bench"><div class="bench-controls"><label>Choose an investigation<select id="vessel-type"><option value="artery">Artery · pulse and wall</option><option value="vein">Vein · valve and return</option><option value="capillary">Capillary · exchange surface</option></select></label><label id="exchange-site-label" hidden>Exchange location<select id="exchange-site"><option value="body">Body tissue</option><option value="lungs">Lungs</option></select></label><button id="back-pressure" aria-pressed="false" hidden>Try reverse pressure</button><button id="pause-flow" aria-pressed="false">Pause blood flow</button><button id="reset-view">Reset view</button></div><div class="lab-columns"><div class="live-column"><div class="vessel-art" id="vessel-art"></div><div class="component-toolbar" aria-label="Choose a blood component">{''.join('<button type="button" data-pick="'+c+'" aria-pressed="false">'+c+'</button>' for c in ['rbc','plasma','wbc','platelets'])}</div><div class="observation-strip" id="vessel-observations"></div><p id="flow-note" class="caption"></p><p id="vessel-reason" class="caption"></p><p class="caption">Inspecting: <strong id="component-name"></strong>. Use these fixed component buttons if a moving cell is hard to select.</p></div><aside class="component-detail" id="component-detail" aria-label="Selected component explanation"></aside></div><div class="model-note">Red blood, even on the return journey. Colours help distinguish components; gas dots are enlarged symbols. Rare white cells and platelets are deliberately overrepresented. Vessel sizes and wall thickness are illustrative.</div></div><div class="quick-check"><h3>Check your understanding · explain the vessel</h3><div id="vessel-question"></div></div></section><section id="blood-composition">{head(3,'HANDLE · SEPARATE · INTERPRET','What is actually in the blood?','Follow the instrument steps. Predict which part will form the largest layer before you spin.')}<div class="bench tube-bench"><div class="tube-step"><span id="tube-step" class="step-tag"></span><p id="tube-instruction" role="status"></p></div><div id="tube-art"></div><div class="tube-control-dock"><div id="tube-actions"></div><button id="tube-help">Help with this step</button><button id="tube-reset">Reset sample</button></div><div class="tube-layers" id="tube-layers" hidden><button data-layer="plasma">Inspect plasma</button><button data-layer="wbc">White cells in the buffy coat</button><button data-layer="platelets">Platelets in the buffy coat</button><button data-layer="rbc">Inspect red-cell layer</button><p id="layer-feedback" role="status"></p></div><p class="caption">A centrifuge separates suspended material using rapid rotation. Denser red cells settle below a thin buffy coat of white cells and platelets. “Buffy” describes the pale layer’s appearance. An anticoagulant prevents clotting; without one, a clot would form and the remaining liquid would be serum. This instrument is already balanced with a matching tube; real blood handling requires trained staff.</p></div><div class="quick-check"><h3>Check your understanding · interpret the layers</h3><fieldset><legend>What do the separated layer heights represent?</legend><label><input type="radio" name="tube-answer" value="volume">Approximate fractions of blood volume.</label><label><input type="radio" name="tube-answer" value="count">The percentage of all cells that are white cells.</label><label><input type="radio" name="tube-answer" value="oxygen">The percentage of oxygen in the blood.</label><button id="check-tube">Check my interpretation</button><p id="tube-feedback" role="status"></p></fieldset></div></section><section id="transport-game">{head(4,'PLAY · DELIVER · EXPLAIN','Transport Dispatch','Choose a carrier for the cargo, set a destination, follow the route and explain the delivery.')}<div class="game-invite"><div><span class="kicker">10 GUIDED MISSIONS · NO TIMER</span><h3>Get the right cargo<br>to the right place.</h3><p>Move oxygen, glucose, carbon-dioxide equivalents, urea, messages and heat. Then tackle immune defence and repair. A wrong turn gives an explanation and a chance to try again.</p><a class="button primary" href="transport-game.html">Open the transport game →</a></div><img src="game-cover.svg" width="900" height="620" alt="Simplified transport map linking lungs, left and right heart, muscle, liver, kidneys, skin and an injury"></div></section><section>{head(5,'CONNECT THE MODEL TO LIFE','Why does this matter?')}<div class="connection-grid"><article><h3>Running for the bus</h3><p>Working muscle uses more oxygen and releases more carbon dioxide and heat. Heart rate and blood distribution change to support demand. A red cell, dissolved bicarbonate and warm plasma contribute different parts of the same response.</p></article><article><h3>A small cut</h3><p>Platelets form a local plug; plasma proteins make a stabilising fibrin mesh. Immune cells and oxygen supply support later defence and repair. One wound therefore needs several components at different stages.</p></article><article><h3>A laboratory blood count</h3><p>A laboratory can measure red cells, white cells and platelets separately. Haematocrit is the fraction of whole-blood volume occupied by red cells. Counts and volume fractions answer different questions; this teaching model cannot diagnose a person.</p></article></div></section>{sources}<section class="revision-invite" id="revision-invitation"><div><span class="kicker">06 / INDEPENDENT REVISION</span><h2>Ready to test your understanding?</h2><p>Choose from 60 multiple-choice questions about blood components, transport, defence, clotting, vessel structure and exchange. Practise with explanations, or take Test yourself and review your answers.</p></div><a class="button primary" href="revision.html">Open revision simulator →</a></section><noscript><p>The explanations above are readable without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript to operate the models and game.</p></noscript>'''
(folder/'index.html').write_text(shell('Inside your blood vessels',body,'lab.js'))
game=f'''<section class="game-hero"><span class="kicker">Y9 SCIENCE · THE BLOOD TRANSPORT GAME</span><h1>Transport Dispatch</h1><p>Deliver what a tissue needs, or take away what it needs to remove. Choose for the <strong>named cargo</strong>: several components can help at the same location.</p></section><section class="how-to" aria-labelledby="how-to"><h2 id="how-to">How to play</h2><ol><li>Read the cargo and starting point. Click a component card, or drag it into the yellow carrier bay.</li><li>Click the destination organ on the map and launch. Follow the highlighted route.</li><li>Answer the explanation question. Retry wrong choices; hints and revealed solutions help you learn.</li></ol><p class="caption">Three stars for an unaided first-time delivery, two after a wrong attempt, one with a hint and zero for a revealed mission. No countdown. Choose Play for my teacher to record game rounds, or Practice to keep them local. Game records are separate from revision tests.</p></section><div class="dispatch-board" id="dispatch-board"><header class="dispatch-top"><div><span class="kicker" id="dispatch-progress"></span><h2 id="mission-title"></h2><p>Cargo: <strong id="mission-cargo"></strong></p></div><span class="dispatch-score" id="dispatch-score"></span></header><div class="mission-brief"><p id="mission-brief"></p><p id="dispatch-step"></p></div><div class="dispatch-workspace"><aside class="carrier-sidebar"><h3>Choose the carrier</h3><div id="carrier-dock" class="carrier-dock"></div><div id="carrier-bay" class="carrier-bay" aria-label="Carrier loading bay"></div><div class="delivery-dock"><p id="route-description"></p><button id="launch-delivery" class="primary" disabled>Launch delivery</button></div></aside><div id="dispatch-map" class="dispatch-map"></div></div><p id="dispatch-feedback" class="dispatch-feedback" role="status"></p><div class="dispatch-explanation"><div id="dispatch-question"></div><button id="dispatch-check" class="primary" hidden>Check my explanation</button><button id="dispatch-next" class="primary" hidden>Next delivery →</button></div><div class="dispatch-tools"><button id="dispatch-hint">Give me a hint</button><button id="dispatch-reveal">Reveal this delivery</button><button id="dispatch-restart">Restart all missions</button><p id="dispatch-hint-text" hidden></p></div></div><section id="dispatch-results" class="game-results" hidden aria-live="polite"></section><section class="game-context"><h2>Read the route, not the drawing’s geography.</h2><p>From a body tissue, blood returns to the right heart, goes to the lungs and then reaches the left heart before another systemic delivery. Systemic means the body circulation outside the lungs. The left-heart starting point means the carrier is already in circulating blood; cells are not being made there. Simplified branch lines may cross on the drawing without forming anatomical junctions.</p><p>Oxygen unloads from haemoglobin; glucose can leave plasma through exchange and membrane-transport routes; carbon dioxide is carried mainly as bicarbonate. The map token represents a delivery travelling <em>within blood</em>, not a whole red cell entering an organ’s tissue. Blood colour is always red, even where return routes are drawn purple.</p><a href="./#learn-first">Revisit component functions and vessel structure →</a></section>{sources}'''
(folder/'transport-game.html').write_text(shell('Transport Dispatch',game,'game.js'))
# Reuse the established revision engine; require a matching server bank before recording.
r=(ROOT/'activities/y7/maths/statistics/revision.html').read_text().replace('Year 7 Maths','Year 9 Science').replace('Y7 Maths','Y9 Science').replace('Y7 Science','Y9 Science').replace('y7/maths','y9/science').replace('statistics','blood-vessels').replace('Statistics','Inside your blood vessels').replace('#/years/y7','#/years/y9')
(folder/'revision.html').write_text(r)
cpath=ROOT/'catalog.json';c=json.loads(cpath.read_text());subject=next(s for y in c['years'] if y['id']=='y9' for s in y['subjects'] if s['id']=='science');topic=dict(id='blood-vessels',name='Blood vessels and blood',appTitle='Inside your blood vessels',description='Discover how vessel walls and blood components support transport, defence and repair.',primaryActivity='blood-vessels-lab',activities=[dict(id='blood-vessels-lab',title='Inside your blood vessels',description='Explore moving blood, vessel structure and component functions; operate a centrifuge and interpret blood composition.',format='Interactive biology lab',duration='25–40 min',href='activities/y9/science/blood-vessels/',image='activities/y9/science/blood-vessels/cover.svg'),dict(id='blood-transport-game',title='Transport Dispatch',description='Choose a blood component, deliver ten different cargos through the circulation and explain each journey.',format='Transport game',duration='10–15 min',href='activities/y9/science/blood-vessels/transport-game.html',image='activities/y9/science/blood-vessels/game-cover.svg'),dict(id='blood-vessels-revision',title='Blood vessels and blood: revision simulator',description='60 questions on blood composition, oxygen and dissolved transport, defence, clotting, vessels and exchange.',format='Revision simulator',duration='Choose your session',href='activities/y9/science/blood-vessels/revision.html',image='activities/y9/science/blood-vessels/cover.svg')])
idx=next((i for i,t in enumerate(subject['topics']) if t['id']==topic['id']),None)
if idx is None:subject['topics'].insert(1,topic)
else:subject['topics'][idx]=topic
cpath.write_text(json.dumps(c,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Built Inside your blood vessels, Transport Dispatch and a separate revision wrapper; Y9 Science catalogue integrated.')

